  gap, and it's the only gap in the plan.

  [1mThe tooling fork.[0m Nothing is configured in this repo today — no scripts, no devDependencies, no runner, no linter.
  Setting these up is cheapest right now, before the code exists, and each one has a different cost profile:

  - [1mUnit tests via [38;5;153mnode:test[0m — built into Node, so [38;5;153mnpm test[39m becomes [38;5;153mnode --test[39m with [3mzero[0m added dependencies. This is
    the one I'd call near-mandatory: it's what makes the persistence claim verifiable at all, and it costs nothing but a
    [38;5;153mscripts[39m entry.
  - [1mESLint + Prettier[0m — catches the real hazards in this kind of code (a typo'd [38;5;153mDEFAULTS[39m key, a stray global) and
    settles formatting permanently. [3mCost:[0m it puts the first [38;5;153mdevDependencies[39m and [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m into a repo that currently
    has none, plus a config declaring browser globals.
  - [1mPlaywright end-to-end[0m — the only way to prove the round-trip in a real browser across a real reload, which is
    exactly what you asked for. [3mCost:[0m the heaviest by far — a dependency that downloads browser binaries, for one
    checkbox on a stub login form.
  - [1mFuzz / mutation testing[0m — I'd skip it. The interesting input surface is "arbitrary junk in one [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m string,"
    and the handful of explicit corruption tests above cover it more legibly than a fuzzer would.

  My recommendation: [1m[38;5;153mnode:test[39m plus ESLint/Prettier[0m, and skip Playwright for now. Unit tests carry the correctness
  argument, linting is cheap insurance at this stage, and Playwright's browser download is hard to justify against
  manually reloading the page once — though it's the honest choice if you want the end-to-end gap actually closed rather
  than acknowledged. Whatever you pick lands in the spec's Global Constraints so the implementation plan inherits it.

